toration-war"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/dominican-restoration-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dominican Restoration War</span><span class="enc-type">War · 1863–1865</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dominican Restoration War was a guerrilla war fought from 1863 to 1865 against Spain's reannexation of the Dominican Republic.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dominican-war-of-independence"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/dominican-war-of-independence.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dominican War of Independence</span><span class="enc-type">War · 1844–1856</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dominican War of Independence was the war by which the Dominican Republic won its independence from Haiti, which had ruled the whole of Hispaniola since 1822.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dominicus-gundissalinus"><span class="enc-name">Dominicus Gundissalinus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dominicus Gundissalinus was a Castilian philosopher and translator, archdeacon of Cuéllar, who worked in Toledo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dominikus-zimmermann"><span class="enc-name">Dominikus Zimmermann</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dominikus Zimmermann was a German architect and stuccoist of the Bavarian rococo, born in Wessobrunn.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dominion"><span class="enc-name">Dominion</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">A dominion was a self-governing territory of the British Empire, such as Canada, Australia, New Zealand and South Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dominique-de-gourgues"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/dominique-de-gourgues.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dominique de Gourgues</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dominique de Gourgues was a French soldier from Gascony.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dominique-mbonyumutwa"><span class="enc-name">Dominique Mbonyumutwa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dominique Mbonyumutwa was a Rwandan Hutu politician and sub-chief.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dominus-et-deus"><span class="enc-name">Dominus et deus</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Dominus et deus ('lord and god') was a title associated with certain Roman emperors, notably Domitian, who is reported to have wished to be addressed in this way.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/domitian"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/domitian.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Domitian</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Domitian was Roman emperor from 81 to 96 CE, the last of the Flavian dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/domitius-alexander"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/domitius-alexander.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Domitius Alexander</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Domitius Alexander was a Roman usurper who proclaimed himself emperor in Africa around 308, rebelling against Maxentius, who ruled Italy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/domitius-domitianus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/domitius-domitianus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Domitius Domitianus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Lucius Domitius Domitianus was a usurper who proclaimed himself emperor in Egypt in 297, probably in reaction to Diocletian's tax reforms.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dommoc"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/dommoc.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dommoc</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dommoc was the seat of the first bishopric of East Anglia, given around 630 by King Sigeberht to the Burgundian missionary Felix.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/domnall-brecc"><span class="enc-name">Domnall Brecc</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Domnall Brecc was a king of Dál Riata of the Cenél nGabráin, grandson of Áedán mac Gabráin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/domnall-mac-aedo"><span class="enc-name">Domnall mac Áedo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Domnall mac Áedo was a king of Cenél Conaill, a branch of the northern Uí Néill, and high king of Ireland, who died in 642.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/domnall-mor-ua-briain"><span class="enc-name">Domnall Mór Ua Briain</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Domnall Mór Ua Briain was king of Thomond and, at times, of Munster from 1168.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/domodossola"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/domodossola.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Domodossola</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Domodossola is a town in Piedmont, northern Italy, the main centre of the Val d'Ossola near the Swiss border, on the road and railway from Milan to the Simplon Pass.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/domoni"><span class="enc-name">Domoni</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Domoni is a town on the east coast of Anjouan in the Comoros.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/domremy-la-pucelle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/domremy-la-pucelle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Domrémy-la-Pucelle</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Domrémy-la-Pucelle is a village on the Meuse in the Vosges department of north-eastern France.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/don-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/don-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Don (river)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Don is a river of south-western Russia, about 1,870 kilometres long, flowing from south of Moscow to the Sea of Azov.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/don-quixote"><span class="enc-name">Don Quixote</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Don Quixote is a novel by Miguel de Cervantes, published in two parts in 1605 and 1615.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/donal-an-chogaidh-o-flaithbheartaigh"><span class="enc-name">Dónal an Chogaidh Ó Flaithbheartaigh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dónal an Chogaidh Ó Flaithbheartaigh, 'Donal of the Battles', was a chieftain of the O'Flaherty lordship of Iar Connacht in western Ireland and heir to its leadership.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/donal-maccarthy-mor-1st-earl-of-clancare"><span class="enc-name">Donal MacCarthy Mór, 1st Earl of Clancare</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Donal MacCarthy Mór was the last MacCarthy Mór, head of the MacCarthy dynasty of Desmond in south-west Ireland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/donald-cameron-colonial-administrator"><span class="enc-name">Donald Cameron (colonial administrator)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</