ermany, at the meeting of the Brigach and Breg, which form the Danube.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/donaustauf"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/donaustauf.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Donaustauf</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Donaustauf is a market town on the Danube east of Regensburg in Bavaria, Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/donbas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/donbas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Donbas</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Donbas is a region of coal and heavy industry in the basin of the Donets River, in present-day eastern Ukraine.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/donegal-castle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/donegal-castle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Donegal Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Donegal Castle stands on the River Eske in Donegal town, Ireland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/donegal-friary"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/donegal-friary.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Donegal Friary</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Donegal Friary was a Franciscan house founded in 1474 by Hugh Roe O'Donnell, lord of Tír Chonaill, and his wife Nuala at Donegal town.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dong-chang"><span class="enc-name">Dong Chang</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dong Chang (died 896) was a late Tang warlord who controlled Yuezhou, in modern Zhejiang, as military governor.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dong-hoi"><span class="enc-name">Dong Hoi</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dong Hoi is a coastal city in central Vietnam, capital of Quang Binh province.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dong-jieyuan"><span class="enc-name">Dong Jieyuan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dong Jieyuan was a writer of the Jin dynasty, active in the late twelfth century, whose real name is unknown; Jieyuan was an honorific title for a top examination…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dong-yi"><span class="enc-name">Dong Yi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dong Yi was a Qin commander who urged Zhang Han to surrender to Xiang Yu in 207 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhuo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/dong-zhuo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dong Zhuo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dong Zhuo was a Chinese general and warlord of the late Han dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dongcheon-of-goguryeo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/dongcheon-of-goguryeo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dongcheon of Goguryeo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dongcheon was the eleventh king of Goguryeo, reigning from 227 to 248.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/donghak"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/donghak.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Donghak</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Donghak ('Eastern Learning') is a Korean religious movement founded in 1860 by Choe Je-u.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/donghak-peasant-revolution"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/donghak-peasant-revolution.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Donghak Peasant Revolution</span><span class="enc-type">Revolt · 1894–1895</span><span class="enc-snip">The Donghak Peasant Revolution was a rising of Korean peasants in 1894, led by followers of the Donghak religion such as Jeon Bong-jun, against corruption, heavy taxes…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/donghu"><span class="enc-name">Donghu</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Donghu ('Eastern Hu') were a nomadic confederation in the steppe east of the Xiongnu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/donglin-movement"><span class="enc-name">Donglin movement</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Donglin movement was a group of Confucian scholar-officials of the late Ming dynasty centred on the Donglin Academy at Wuxi, revived by Gu Xiancheng in 1604.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dongmaeng"><span class="enc-name">Dongmaeng</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Dongmaeng was a great state festival of Goguryeo held in the tenth month of the year to give thanks to Heaven, recorded in the Chinese Records of the Three Kingdoms.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dongmyeong-of-buyeo"><span class="enc-name">Dongmyeong of Buyeo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dongmyeong is the legendary founder of Buyeo, an ancient kingdom of Manchuria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dongmyeong-of-goguryeo"><span class="enc-name">Dongmyeong of Goguryeo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dongmyeong, known as Jumong, was the founder of Goguryeo, traditionally in 37 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/donika-kastrioti"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/donika-kastrioti.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Donika Kastrioti</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Donika Kastrioti, born Andronika Arianiti, was the daughter of the Albanian nobleman Gjergj Arianiti and the wife of Skanderbeg, whom she married in 1451.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/donn-carrach-maguire"><span class="enc-name">Donn Carrach Maguire</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Donn Carrach Maguire is counted as the first Maguire king of Fermanagh, ruling from about 1264 until his death in 1302.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/donnchad-mac-gilla-patraic"><span class="enc-name">Donnchad mac Gilla Pátraic</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Donnchad mac Gilla Pátraic was king of Osraige from 1003 and King of Leinster from 1033 until his death in 1039.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/donnchad-ua-cerbaill"><span class="enc-name">Donnchad Ua Cerbaill</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Donnchad Ua Cerbaill was king of Airgialla in the north of Ireland in the 12th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/donnuban-mac-cathail"><span class="enc-name">Donnubán mac Cathail</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Donnubán mac Cathail was king of the Uí Fidgenti, in what is now County Limerick, in the later 10th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/donnus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/donnus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Donnus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Donnus was a king of the Alpine peoples around Segusio (Susa) and the Montgenèvre pass in the 1st century BCE, in the time of Julius Caesar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/donoughmore-constitution"><span class="enc-name">Donoughmore Constitution</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Donoughmore Constitution was the constitution of British Ceylon from 1931 to 1947, based on the report of a commission led by the Earl of Donoughmore.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/donyo-dorje"><span class="enc-name">Donyö Dorje</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Donyö Dorje was the most powerful ruler of the Rinpungpa dynasty of Tsang in central Tibet.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dooars"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/dooars.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dooars</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dooars are the alluvial plains along the southern foot of the eastern Himalayas, in present-day West Bengal and Assam, India, which control the passes into Bhutan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/doon-valley"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/doon-valley.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Doon Valley</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Doon Valley is a wide valley in the Indian state of Uttarakhand, lying between the Sivalik Hills and the Lesser Himalaya and bounded by the Ganges and Yamuna rivers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dora-baltea"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/dora-baltea.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dora Baltea</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dora Baltea is a river of north-western Italy that rises below Mont Blanc and flows through the Aosta Valley and past Ivrea to join the Po.</span></a></li></ul>
